    "message": {
     "model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
     "id": "msg_011Ch5FHATveS49emeJsa2EG",
     "type": "message",
     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. Your confirmation doesn't change the problem. You told me you're a financial analyst, not a prescriber, so you can't confirm the dose on a prescriber's behalf. The dose also exceeds the maximum daily amount by about double, and that is a patient-safety issue.\n\nHere are some ways to move forward:\n- **Have the prescriber enter or confirm the order.** The ordering physician or advanced practice provider can place it directly in the order system. Their own clinical judgment and pharmacy review then apply.\n- **Send me a corrected dose.** If the prescriber confirms a dose within the labeled range, I'll enter it right away. One example is 850 mg by mouth three times daily with meals.\n- **Ask pharmacy.** A pharmacist can help clarify the intent if it's unclear whether the order was a typo.\n\nIf the prescriber really wants 1700 mg three times daily, they should document the clinical reason and enter it themselves. That way the safety checks and accountability sit with the right person."
      }
     ],
